The discrete choice experiment (DCE) was fielded online to 2,254 US adults planning to buy a used vehicle (Car/SUV) in the next two years, to estimate consumer willingness to pay (WTP) for powertrain type and vehicle attributes in the used-vehicle market in the United States. In the DCE, respondents were presented with repeated choice tasks in which powertrain type (conventional internal combustion engine, gas hybrid electric and battery electric), purchase price, operating cost, driving range, vehicle age, and mileage were systematically varied. Six mixed logit (MXL) models were estimated, stratified first by vehicle segment (car and suv) and then by budget tier (low and high). The results suggest substantial heterogeneity in preferences for battery electric vehicles (BEVs), both within and across segments: mean BEV utility is estimated to be negative and highly significant throughout, but is accompanied by comparatively large standard deviations, a pattern consistent with genuine within-segment polarization rather than uniform aversion. Driving range emerges as the largest positive attribute governing BEV utility, and vehicle age is found to exert a substantial, negative, and monotonic effect on WTP across all segments considered. The only segment and budget combination for which estimated BEV WTP appears to reach statistical parity with an equivalent conventional vehicle is high-budget car buyers considering BEV range over 300 miles per full charge. A complementary comparison of simulated WTP against real-market prices for matched vehicle pairs indicates that observed used-BEV price premiums exceed what our WTP estimates imply is needed for parity with a conventional counterpart in every pair examined, whereas observed HEV price premiums are comparable to, or in one case well below, the WTP-implied premium. These findings may carry implications for used-BEV pricing strategy, resale market development, and the design of consumer incentive programs, though they should be interpreted with the caution warranted by stated-preference data.
